fact WorkItem(id: "WI-612", description: some(value: "Typer (abstract-E effect threading, CARRIER-PARAM CONSUMER variant): a qualified Iterable consumer (isEmpty / find) over a bare Stream receiver whose access row E is ABSTRACT (unwritten) leaks the effect as '?_' instead of threading it as the receiver's projection 's.E'. Reproducer: 'operation f(s: Stream[T = Int64]) -> Bool effects s.E = Iterable.isEmpty(s)' fails to load with 'f.effects: expected declared [s.E], got undeclared effect: ?_' (find leaks the same). Distinct from WI-604 (which fixed the CONCRETE case: a bare Stream with WRITTEN E={}, e.g. Iterable.map on a List, dispatched-override projection s.E now grounds to {}). Here E is UNWRITTEN, so the carrier-param binding (bind_spec_params_from_carrier_param via parameterized_short_bindings) finds no E type-arg on 'Stream[T=Int64]' -> Iterable.E stays unbound -> '?_'; for it to thread, the abstract receiver's E must be represented as the self-projection 's.E' (the param's own E) and Iterable.E bound to it. Also note the dispatched Stream.isEmpty override contributes a second 's.E' (WI-604 fallback re-key), so f_bad shows BOTH '?_' and 's.E'. FOUND during WI-604 (verified IDENTICAL before/after that change - not a WI-604 regression). Related to the WI-608/609 abstract-E family but a DISTINCT reproducer (bare Stream param + carrier-param Iterable consumer, vs their FiniteCollection-field receivers). ACCEPTANCE: 'Iterable.isEmpty(s)' / 'Iterable.find(s, p)' on 's: Stream[T=Int64]' (abstract E) type-checks with the incurred effect threaded as 's.E' (matching a declared 'effects s.E'); a focused typer test + cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-02T15:41:48Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-612", name: "finiteness")
